tive Health Sciences, Lerner Research Institute, Cleveland Clinic, 9500 Euclid Avenue/JJN3-01, Cleveland, OH 44195 USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Gerds TA", "lastName": "Gerds", "firstName": "Thomas A", "initials": "TA", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "2Department of Public Health, Section of Biostatistics, University of Copenhagen, N.J. Fjords Alle 12, 4 th 1957 Frederiksberg, Øster Farimagsgade 5, 1014 Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"volume": "2", "journalIssueId": 2744744, "dateOfPublication": "2018 ", "monthOfPublication": 0, "yearOfPublication": 2018, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Diagn Progn Res", "title": "Diagnostic and prognostic research", "essn": "2397-7523", "issn": "2397-7523", "isoabbreviation": "Diagn Progn Res", "nlmid": "101718985"}, "printPublicationDate": "2018-01-01"}, "pubYear": "2018", "pageInfo": "7", "abstractText": "<h4>Background</h4>Many measures of prediction accuracy have been developed. However, the most popular ones in typical medical outcome prediction settings require additional investigation of calibration.<h4>Methods</h4>We show how rescaling the Brier score produces a measure that combines discrimination and calibration in one value and improves interpretability by adjusting for a benchmark model. We have called this measure the index of prediction accuracy (IPA). The IPA permits a common interpretation across binary, time to event, and competing risk outcomes. We illustrate this measure using example datasets.<h4>Results</h4>The IPA is simple to compute, and example code is provided. The values of the IPA appear very interpretable.<h4>Conclusions</h4>IPA should be a prominent measure reported in studies of medical prediction model performance. However, IPA is only a measure of average performance and, by default, does not measure the utility of a medical decision.", "affiliation": "1Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Lerner Research Institute, Cleveland Clinic, 9500 Euclid Avenue/JJN3-01, Cleveland, OH 44195 USA.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic-eCollection", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Journal Article"]}, "keywordList": {"keyword": ["Prediction", "Accuracy", "Brier Score"]}, "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Unpaywall", "url": "https://diagnprognres.biomedcentral.com/track/pdf/10.1186/s41512-018-0029-2"}, {"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s41512-018-0029-2"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC6460739"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC6460739?pdf=render"}]}, "isOpenAccess": "Y", "inEPMC": "Y", "inPMC": "Y", "citedByCount": 103, "hasReferences": "Y", "hasTextMinedTerms": "Y", "hasDbCrossReferences": "N", "hasPDF": "Y", "hasBook": "N", "hasSuppl": "Y", "hasLabsLinks": "Y", "hasData": "Y", "license": "cc by", "authMan": "N", "epmcAuthMan": "N", "nihAuthMan": "N", "hasTMAccessionNumbers": "N", "dateOfCreation": "2019-05-17", "dateOfRevision": "2022-04-08", "electronicPublicationDate": "2018-05-04", "firstPublicationDate": "2018-05-04", "firstIndexDate": "2019-05-17", "fullTextReceivedDate": "2025-02-03", "hasEvaluations": "N"}]}}